at we have so right  off the bat I can see that I have four  people that are relevant and are  influential but I'm not following them  so if I want to be in that conversation  I need to start by following those  people I can look at how often they're  talking about things that are relevant  to me if I decide that I'm going to  let's see if I want to talk specifically  about climate action so I want to talk  about people who are interested in  oceans and climate action I can go down  and find the user who most frequently  uses climate action along with ocean  information I can see what hashtags  they're using that are relevant to what  we're interested in as well as the  overall hashtags that are being used and  so I know that we've run through this  very quickly and we will post the some  of these examples online at the end but  the idea for us is that a lot of the  examples we've seen and using the  Twitter service connect have been  one-dimensional let me download text  language location and get X or let me  download all the tweets for this user  and analyze this but what we've seen is  that when you start crossing those  things over you start looking at  multiple service requests that you can  cross-reference with each other that you  can add to your own analytics and  visualization you can actually start  building tools that you can sit down  figure out how you can tweet better how  can you how can you incorporate best  practices that you've learned who can  you be talking with that is  most likely to retweet you how can we  get into a space where we're regularly  engaging on the hashtags that are  important to us now given that this is  only a 20 minute presentation at best we  have already stripped out a whole lot of  the work that we've done but we'd be  more than happy to talk with you about  what those elements are in a excusing in  addition to what we've we've shown you  here there's actually a couple other  things that we've done when we're  working with 